nel.sh`.**  OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-10-01 ("Skip any 5.6 SOL just use Astra if using Codex; if Astra AUP fails gpt-daybreak-blue-latest"): the codex seat is GPT-6 Astra ONLY -- never 5.6-SOL -- and `gpt-daybreak-blue-latest` is fired only when Astra returns a content-filter (AUP) refusal; `tools/cxlaunch.sh` re-runs a refused brief once on it. ***  Re-check the model name after any further CLI upgrade -- a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE. *** | `codex exec --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check -m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh" "$(cat brief)" < /dev/null` (the 5.6-SOL form until 2026-09-04) — the `< /dev/null` is required. Windows argv caps ~32 KB. *** `panel.sh` NOW HANDLES THIS ITSELF, as of 2026-08-30 -- do NOT hand-trim a brief for it. *** Above `ARGV_CAP` (30000) codex and kimi are fed a POINTER (`PTR_PROMPT`: read `$BRIEF_ABS` and review it as its own Questions section directs) instead of the contents; below it the inline form is byte-unchanged. Both are FILE-READING seats, so this costs nothing -- and grok (`--prompt-file`) and agy (`--add-dir`) were already immune, leaving these two as the only argv-fed seats. **The block previously WARNED and then launched anyway**, which cost both seats on a 38 KB brief and is the check-that-cannot-fail family's sibling: *a message reporting an ACTION the code does not take*, exactly like the re-firing message. `PANEL_ARGV_CAP` overrides the cap **only so the branch is testable in both directions** without keeping a 38 KB fixture; it is not a tuning knob. Controlled by extracting the real decision block out of `panel.sh` and `eval`ing it -- 6/6 rc=0 fixed, EXTRACT FAILED rc=9 pre-fix. **A HAND-WRITTEN BRIEF STILL NEEDS TO BE READABLE**: the pointer form only helps seats that can open the file, so it is not licence to grow briefs without limit -- the packet-fed Ollama seats still get the whole thing POSTed and still burn budget on it. Never the trailing-`-` stdin form (died silently once). |
