# `#577` (2026-11-02) -- `shblreset`: a SuperH general exception taken with SR.BL set stored the power-on reset code, and clobbered the TLB multiple-hit code

**The defect.**  `src/cpus/cpu_sh.c`, `sh_exception()` (HEAD `987cb596` :910-918): when SR.BL was already set the block printed its
`fatal()` and then executed `expevt = EXPEVT_RESET_POWER;` for EVERY exception code, under a `/* TODO */`.  The SH7750 manual
(`_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt`) says a general exception other than a user break taken with BL set performs a MANUAL reset -- 5.5.3
`:12302-12304`, 5.6.1 (2) `:12376` -- and "Exception code H'020 is set in EXPEVT" (`:12383-12384`; 5.7.2.a `:13164-13166` repeats it as
H'0000 0020), not the power-on code H'000; and the two TLB multiple-hit exceptions are reset-category in their own right (Table 5.2
`:12006`, `:12010`) with their own code H'140 (`:12461` instruction side, `:12499` data side), which the block overwrote too -- so the
`#457` guest-distinguishability comment at `:985-989` ("a multiple hit can be told from a power-on (0x000) or a manual reset (0x020)")
was false under BL.  Guest-visible consequence: a kernel's reset handler dispatching on EXPEVT read 0x000 for a manual reset and for a
multiple hit alike.  Upstream residue (the TODO is the 0.7.0 import's).  WRONG-RESULT, MED; no host failure path (`fatal()` only prints,
`debugmsg.c:384-391`).  Two carriers in the bug file: the id entry `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:24063` and the prose row `:8238`.

**The witness (rung 3).**  `regress/artifacts/shblreset/witness_20261102/sh_blreset_witness.py` on the unmodified in-tree `-E landisk`
(the rig kernel loaded for construction only), the cold debugger presetting `sr` (MD|BL = 0x500000f0, RB clear) and `pc`, the guest
executing `trapa #1` (0xc301) or the TLB multiple-hit raiser's path, `expevt`/`pc` read back through the debugger.  On a fresh clone of
HEAD built in WSL (`/tmp/gxdivw`, binary newer than source): `witness_head.out` RESULT=2/4 -- both failures read H'000 against the wanted
H'020 and H'140 -- with the liveness control (`bl trapa pc` 0xa0000000, the reset vector) and the BL-clear control (`nobl trapa expevt`
0x160) green.  The same script on the fixed clone (`/tmp/gxfix577`, 17:47): `witness_fixed.out` RESULT=4/4.

**The fix.**  In the BL block, `expevt = EXPEVT_RESET_POWER;` becomes `if (expevt != EXPEVT_RESET_TLB_MULTI_HIT) expevt =
EXPEVT_RESET_MANUAL;`, and the TODO comment becomes a twelve-line `#577` comment carrying the manual citations above, the reason the
multiple-hit code is exempt, why a user break never reaches the block (the UBC suppresses it under BL, `:41448-41452`, and
`EXPEVT_BREAK` has no raiser in this tree -- `sh4_exception.h:73` is its only occurrence), and why an interrupt cannot latch a code here
(`cpu_dyntrans.c:247` delivers none while BL is set; the intevt path at `:945-947` never stores EXPEVT for an interrupt).  Nothing else
in `sh_exception()` changes: the `fatal()` stays (its flood is `floodclass`, not this round's), the common tail and the shared reset arm
(`:1015-1021`) are untouched.  Option set: IMPLEMENT the manual's rule -- the arm already existed and only its constant was wrong; there
is nothing to DECLINE or FAULT (the exception is legal and the manual says exactly what happens), and DELETE would leave the guest's own
code in EXPEVT where the architecture mandates H'020.  Diff: `src/cpus/cpu_sh.c` +14 -5 (`killtable_20261102/product.diff`, the
shipping text; `witness_20261102/product.diff` and its `apply_fix577.py` carry the pre-pass-1 wording of the SAME code -- comment-only,
and both builds share the binary md5 `b7b0d0b5f5ae` at 17:47 and 18:11), byte-identical in `GXEMUL-SEC` and `est/`.  Every caller of
`sh_exception()` at HEAD was enumerated at pass 2 (Fable; `git grep`): no caller passes H'000 or H'020 as an exception, so the exempt set
is exactly {H'140} and every value the fix stores has an arm in the switch -- the fix opens no path to the `default: ... exit(1)` at
`:1083-1085`.

**The detector.**  `regress/sh_tlbmulti_probe.py`: `session()` gains `program=`/`steps=` (a short guest program run under a preset `sr`),
and four rows are appended -- `tlbm bl trapa` (sr 0x500000f0, `trapa #1`, want 0x00000020), `tlbm bl mhit` (the multiple hit under BL,
want 0x00000140), `tlbm bl ldmis` (`mov.l @r1,r2` = 0x6212 with r1 odd under BL: the misaligned-load code H'0E0 is rewritten, want
0x00000020) and `tlbm trapa` (sr 0x400000f0, BL clear, want 0x00000160: the control that pins the general vector's own code and, measured
post-table, the sole carrier of the guard-side class).  Names stay at 13 characters or fewer (`row()` prints `%-14s`, the gate anchors
`^$v  .*ok$` with two spaces: the padded-column trap, Grok pass 1).  The docstring's "rewrites ANY expevt" note is historicised.
Contract (the probe's `#577` comment, final text after three post-table comment-only edits logged in `ASRUN_NOTE.txt`): TRAPA (H'160)
and a misaligned load (H'0E0) are the two general codes driven; any edit that moves a code between the rewritten set and the exempt set
ONLY at a code this table does not drive -- H'040-H'0C0, H'100, H'120, H'180, H'1A0, H'800, H'820 -- in either direction is clause-(e)
disclosure; the identity medium is the REWRITE deleted (the guard and its `fatal()` stay), carried by `tlbm bl trapa` and `tlbm bl ldmis`;
`tlbm bl mhit` reads H'140 either way and cannot carry it.  `regress/gate_sh_rounding.sh`: `sh_tlbmulti rows run` 6 -> 10 (EQUALITY),
the four names in the per-row loop.

**The pre-table check and the kill table** (`regress/artifacts/shblreset/killtable_20261102/`; scratch clones only, a `.MUTANT` sentinel
per mutant; each `<leg>.raw` names its binary, md5, freshness and -- for the post-table legs -- the instrument's md5):

    base_head (HEAD)                                              7/10   bl trapa, bl mhit, bl ldmis all 0x000
    fixed                                                        10/10
    M_b        the power-on store reinstated verbatim (b)         7/10   the same three (binary md5 == HEAD's)
    M_c        the rewrite deleted, guard and fatal() kept (c)    8/10   bl trapa 0x160, bl ldmis 0x0e0
    M_noex     exemption removed (d, body)                        9/10   bl mhit 0x020
    M_wrongex  exemption on TRAPA instead of the multiple hit     8/10   bl trapa 0x160, bl mhit 0x020
    M_lt100    exemption widened to every code below 0x100        8/10   bl mhit 0x020, bl ldmis 0x0e0  (predicted 9/10; corrected)
    M_trapaonly the rewrite keyed to TRAPA only (e)               9/10   bl ldmis 0x0e0
    M_power    exemption kept, constant wrong (d)                 8/10   bl trapa, bl ldmis 0x000
    M_guard    the guard widened to MD as well as BL (d, GUARD)   9/10   trapa 0x020   -- post-table leg, predicted 9/10
    M_resinst  `&& expevt != EXPEVT_RES_INST` added (e)          10/10   TOTAL ESCAPE  -- post-table leg, predicted 10/10

Floor: (a) 7/10; (b) 7/10 at the binary level (the reinstatement rebuilds HEAD byte for byte); (c): measured at `M_c.raw`; (d) the fix
DEPENDS ON the dominating predicate `sr & SH_SR_BL` and on the exemption it adds -- body side M_noex, M_wrongex, M_lt100, M_power, guard
side M_guard (built after the table at Fable's pass-2 ask: until it ran, `tlbm trapa`'s kill was a prediction and that row is the class's
sole carrier; it matched in score, row and value); (e) the contract above, with M_resinst as the MEASURED member of the disclosed class
(all three pass-2 seats named it independently, each with a different undriven code: ADDR_ERR_ST, RES_INST, TLB_MISS); (f) not applicable
(no address decode).  Provenance: `PRETABLE.out` (18:10:13) precedes `KILLTABLE.log`'s start (18:11:05) and was never edited after;
every correction and both post-table predictions went to the SIBLING file `PRETABLE_CORRECTION.out` -- which was then rewritten at
each addendum, so its mtime proves only that the last write followed both legs (the F2 pass caught a self-referential mtime clause in
it; corrected in place, quoting it).  The post-table predictions are pre-leg by other artifacts: the three pass-2 answers that named
the guard-side and the undriven-code classes (`argon/gemini_web.out` 18:43:04, `grok.txt` 18:53:22, `fable5.md` 19:01:50, the last
carrying M_guard's "9/10, tlbm trapa reads 0x00000020" verbatim) precede `M_guard.raw` 19:14:12 and `M_resinst.raw` 19:24:09; so do
`run_mguard577.sh` 19:10:42 and the mutator's M_resinst branch 19:20:21.  Lesson, structural: one file per pre-leg prediction, never
rewritten -- the lesson `#576` paid for the same day, learned a second time.  The M_lt100 prediction had the
direction of the sub-0x100 exemption wrong (the correction quotes it); every other prediction matched.  The instrument the table ran is
preserved as `sh_tlbmulti_probe.ASRUN.py` (copied at run time, md5 `d41edccdee1c`, the file both post-table legs ran); the shipped
`sh_tlbmulti_probe.py` differs from it by three comment passages (`ASRUN_NOTE.txt`: the H'020 citation `:678-681` -> `:12383-12384`,
Grok pass 2; the contract's two directions and "the REWRITE deleted", Fable pass 2; the `sr=` sentence, Fable pass 2) and by nothing
else -- the two files' ASTs are identical once docstrings are dropped (ten `row()` calls each, checked 19:2x).  The durable copies of the
table files were first made with plain `cp` (every mtime 18:40) and were re-copied with `cp -p` from the byte-identical `_scratchpad/w577`
originals at 19:2x, so they now carry the originals' mtimes; Argon's F2 objection that a restored mtime is indistinguishable from a
fabricated one is recorded here, and the `_scratchpad/w577` copies remain the primary order proof.  The mutator's `M_guard` branch first
landed with a real newline inside a C string (the heredoc backslash trap, `#576`'s P1 shape), was caught by `ast.parse` before any run and
repaired with the editor; the preserved `mutate577.py` parses and its seven table branches are byte-unchanged.  No dead session in any
leg.

**The panel** (owner roster 2026-11-02: GPT-6 Astra, Fable 5.1, glm-5.3, Argon = Gemini web, Grok 4.7).  Pass 1
(`_scratchpad/w577/pass1_20261102/`): Argon PROCEED (9 attacks, 9 holds); Grok 4.7 PROCEED WITH CHANGES (3); Fable 5.1 PROCEED WITH
CHANGES (3); glm-5.3 SEAT DOWN (the Ollama cloud endpoint answers `403 Forbidden, error code 1010` to every model; a 70-byte error file);
